\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A finite set $P\subset\mathbb R^2$ is in general position if no three of its points are collinear. A subset $Q\subseteq P$ is in convex position if each of its points is a vertex of $\operatorname{conv}(Q)$, its convex hull. Thus the points of $Q$, when $|Q|=n\ge3$, form a convex $n$-gon; other points of $P$ are allowed inside that polygon.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every integer $n\ge3$, determine the least integer $g(n)$ such that every $g(n)$-point set $P\subset\mathbb R^2$ in general position contains a subset $Q\subseteq P$ with $|Q|=n$ in convex position. Equivalently, every point of $Q$ must be a vertex of $\operatorname{conv}(Q)$. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.08657v2}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
How many points with no three collinear guarantee that some n of them are the vertices of a convex polygon?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 1379 (GEOM-020). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Andrew Suk, \emph{On the Erdős--Szekeres convex polygon problem} (2016), arXiv:1604.08657v2, Section 1, p. 1 (the quantity denoted $\operatorname{ES}(n)$ there). \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.08657v2}.
\end{description}
